{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "MosaicAstemLiquidSurfaceEquilibrium",
    "authors": [
      "EqWeFiC"
    ],
    "description": "The PHYSICS of MOSAIC's gas-particle partitioning of the semi-volatile inorganic gases HNO3, HCl and NH3 onto ONE liquid (or CaCO3-bearing) size bin: the equilibrium vapour concentration each gas has at the particle surface, and the instantaneous condensation rate kg (gas - surface) that follows from it. It is what SUBROUTINE ASTEM_flux_wet and its cases 1-4 (chem/module_mosaic_therm.F, WRF v4.8.0) compute before ASTEM integrates anything; ASTEM's integrator (its semi-implicit sub-step, the adaptive step choice of ASTEM_calculate_dtmax, the 1% equilibrium test that skips a bin, the 200-step cap) is deliberately NOT here. EqWeFiC writes rates and constraints; EarthSciIntegrator integrates them.\n\nINPUTS. The gases and their mass-transfer coefficients kg (gas_particle_mass_transfer.esm), the Kelvin factors (aerosol_phase_state.esm), the liquid ions that set the sulfate ratio X_T (template aerosol_xt), the ion molalities, activity coefficients and electrolyte activities of compute_activities (mtem_activities.esm) and the equilibrium constants (mosaic_gas_equilibrium_constants.esm).\n\nTHE FOUR SURFACES. Which equilibrium holds at the surface depends on the particle:\n  1. solid CaCO3 present: the particle is a perfect acid sink. HNO3 and HCl have zero surface concentration (each only above 1e-5 nmol/m3, as the Fortran writes it) and condense into the SOLID; NH3 does not exchange.\n  2. sulfate-rich, 0 <= X_T < 1.9999: each gas is in Henry's-law equilibrium with the solution separately -- [HNO3]s = kel [H+][NO3-] gamma_HNO3^2 / K_HNO3, [HCl]s likewise, and [NH3]s = kel gamma_ratio [NH4+] K_w / ([H+] K_b K_H,NH3).\n  3. sulfate-poor with ammonia: the surface holds NH4NO3 and NH4Cl in solution equilibrium, [NH3]s [HNO3]s = K_NH4NO3 and [NH3]s [HCl]s = K_NH4Cl (K = kel kel a_salt kp), and the three fluxes carry no net charge, kg_NH3 (g_NH3 - [NH3]s) = kg_HNO3 (g_HNO3 - [HNO3]s) + kg_HCl (g_HCl - [HCl]s). Eliminating the acids gives kg_NH3 [NH3]s^2 + b [NH3]s + c = 0, whose larger root is [NH3]s. This is an exact algebraic solution of the two constraints, not an iteration; FUNCTION quadratic's series form for |4ac/b^2| < 1e-6 is kept, as mtem_activities.esm keeps compute_activities'. H+ is then diagnosed from [HNO3]s (or [HCl]s) through Henry's law.\n  4. sulfate-poor without ammonia: HNO3 and HCl share one H+, so [HNO3]s/[HCl]s is fixed by the solution, and the two fluxes balance, kg_HNO3 (g_HNO3 - [HNO3]s) + kg_HCl (g_HCl - [HCl]s) = 0. Without both nitrate and chloride in solution nothing exchanges and H+ is sqrt(K_w).\nWhere a bin does not exchange a gas its surface value is the gas value itself, so its rate is zero.\n\nTHE RATES. cond_x = kg_x (g_x - [x]s) per bin, nmol/m3/s, positive into the particle; a consumer subtracts the sum over bins from the gas and adds each bin's share to its liquid (to its solid in case 1). In case 2-4 this is the same flux ASTEM forms: its linearised surface value Heff aer_l equals [x]s at the state the fluxes are formed from, because Heff = [x]s/aer_l by construction.\n\nNOT TRANSCRIBED. (a) The 'tiny' adjustments ASTEM_flux_wet makes before cases 3 and 4 (absorb_tiny_nh4no3/nh4cl/hno3/hcl move about 1e-6 of a gas into an empty solution so that its activity is defined): they are state edits, so the tests feed the state AFTER them. (b) equilibrate_acids, the instantaneous gas-liquid projection case 3 falls back to when the solution holds neither nitrate nor chloride: a state rewrite; flagged by acids_equilibrate, and not asserted. (c) Heff, the phi relative driving forces and the integrate flags, which only steer ASTEM's integrator.\n\nReference: the ASTEM tape of the chem_opt = 170 column (fork branch earthsciml-instrumented-chem170; data/eqwefic/notes/chem170_mosaic_astem_capture.md), the real(kind=8) values the Fortran held at every flux call of every ASTEM sub-step, rel 1e-9.",
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Zaveri, R.A., Easter, R.C., Peters, L.K., Wexler, A.S. (2004). Adaptive step time-split Euler method (ASTEM) for gas-particle partitioning. JGR, and Zaveri et al. (2008), MOSAIC, J. Geophys. Res. 113, D13204."
      },
      {
        "citation": "WRF v4.8.0 chem/module_mosaic_therm.F, ASTEM_flux_wet and ASTEM_flux_wet_case1/2/3/4, FUNCTION quadratic"
      }
    ]
  },
  "models": {
